  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Total federal obligations to Oregon are $1.7B for FY2026 to date. The state received 8,308 awards with an average award size of $201K. Obligations are down 20.0% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $924M so far this fiscal year, followed by the Department of Agriculture at $228M and the Department of Transportation at $139M. VIGOR MARINE LLC is the largest vendor, receiving $567M in obligations. Ship Building and Repairing is the largest industry by obligations at $583M, followed by Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $150M. Recent fiscal-year history shows obligations peaked at $2.5B in FY23 and have moved lower since then: FY21 was $1.8B, FY22 was $1.9B, FY23 was $2.5B, FY24 was $2.4B, FY25 was $2.1B, FY26 is $1.7B. These figures are contract obligations and the current-year totals are partial, covering the fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are federal obligations across agencies in Oregon?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense leads with $924M in obligations. The Department of Agriculture and the Department of Transportation follow with $228M and $139M respectively. The remaining top buyers include the Department of Veterans Affairs at $110M and the Department of the Interior at $66M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which vendors and industries dominate federal spending in Oregon?",
        "body": "VIGOR MARINE LLC is the largest vendor at $567M, with COLLINS ELBIT VISION SYSTEMS, LLC and GE RENEWABLES US LLC at $70.9M and $67.6M. Ship Building and Repairing is the largest industry at $583M, followed by Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $150M and Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing at $93.5M."
      }
    ]
  },
